#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:1
msgid "sprintf.js"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:2
msgid ""
"**sprintf.js** is a complete open source JavaScript sprintf "
"implementation for the *browser* and *node.js*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:4
msgid "Its prototype is simple:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:8
msgid ""
"The placeholders in the format string are marked by `%` and are followed "
"by one or more of these elements, in this order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:10
msgid ""
"An optional number followed by a `$` sign that selects which argument "
"index to use for the value. If not specified, arguments will be placed in"
" the same order as the placeholders in the input string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:11
msgid ""
"An optional `+` sign that forces to preceed the result with a plus or "
"minus sign on numeric values. By default, only the `-` sign is used on "
"negative numbers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:12
msgid ""
"An optional padding specifier that says what character to use for padding"
" (if specified). Possible values are `0` or any other character precedeed"
" by a `'` (single quote). The default is to pad with *spaces*."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:13
msgid ""
"An optional `-` sign, that causes sprintf to left-align the result of "
"this placeholder. The default is to right-align the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:14
msgid ""
"An optional number, that says how many characters the result should have."
" If the value to be returned is shorter than this number, the result will"
" be padded. When used with the `j` (JSON) type specifier, the padding "
"length specifies the tab size used for indentation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:15
msgid ""
"An optional precision modifier, consisting of a `.` (dot) followed by a "
"number, that says how many digits should be displayed for floating point "
"numbers. When used with the `g` type specifier, it specifies the number "
"of significant digits. When used on a string, it causes the result to be "
"truncated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:16
msgid "A type specifier that can be any of:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:17
msgid "`%` — yields a literal `%` character"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:18
msgid "`b` — yields an integer as a binary number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:19
msgid "`c` — yields an integer as the character with that ASCII value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:20
msgid "`d` or `i` — yields an integer as a signed decimal number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:21
msgid "`e` — yields a float using scientific notation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:22
msgid "`u` — yields an integer as an unsigned decimal number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:23
msgid "`f` — yields a float as is; see notes on precision above"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:24
msgid "`g` — yields a float as is; see notes on precision above"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:25
msgid "`o` — yields an integer as an octal number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:26
msgid "`s` — yields a string as is"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:27
msgid "`x` — yields an integer as a hexadecimal number (lower-case)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:28
msgid "`X` — yields an integer as a hexadecimal number (upper-case)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:29
msgid "`j` — yields a JavaScript object or array as a JSON encoded string"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:31
msgid "JavaScript `vsprintf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:32
msgid ""
"`vsprintf` is the same as `sprintf` except that it accepts an array of "
"arguments, rather than a variable number of arguments:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:36
msgid "Argument swapping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:37
msgid ""
"You can also swap the arguments. That is, the order of the placeholders "
"doesn't have to match the order of the arguments. You can do that by "
"simply indicating in the format string which arguments the placeholders "
"refer to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:40
msgid ""
"And, of course, you can repeat the placeholders without having to "
"increase the number of arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:42
msgid "Named arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:43
msgid ""
"Format strings may contain replacement fields rather than positional "
"placeholders. Instead of referring to a certain argument, you can now "
"refer to a certain key within an object. Replacement fields are "
"surrounded by rounded parentheses - `(` and `)` - and begin with a "
"keyword that refers to a key:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:49
msgid ""
"Keywords in replacement fields can be optionally followed by any number "
"of keywords or indexes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:57
msgid "Note: mixing positional and named placeholders is not (yet) supported"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:59
msgid "Computed values"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:60
msgid ""
"You can pass in a function as a dynamic value and it will be invoked "
"(with no arguments) in order to compute the value on-the-fly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:65
msgid "AngularJS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/sprintf-js_README.md:66
msgid ""
"You can now use `sprintf` and `vsprintf` (also aliased as `fmt` and "
"`vfmt` respectively) in your AngularJS projects. See `demo/`."
msgstr ""

